<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santería</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Santería is an Afro-Cuban religion that developed among enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a> and their descendants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, blending Yoruba spiritual traditions with elements of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. It centres on the worship of deities known as orishas. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> list Santería, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candomble">Candomblé</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Vodou</a>, as a living legacy of the Yoruba diaspora created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santeria.jpg" alt="Santería" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jorge Royan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Havana_-_Cuba_-_0616.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
